WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.014
[Cameraman] Can you tell 
when people have accents?

00:00:01.014 --> 00:00:02.056
[Cece] Absolutely.

00:00:02.056 --> 00:00:03.725
Oh, you got a strong one.

00:00:03.725 --> 00:00:05.518
[both laughing]

00:00:05.518 --> 00:00:07.186
♩♩

00:00:10.273 --> 00:00:12.066
Hi, my name is Cece Bell, 

00:00:12.066 --> 00:00:17.113
and El Deafo is
the story of my childhood

00:00:17.113 --> 00:00:20.742
and adjusting to
a sudden hearing loss.

00:00:22.285 --> 00:00:24.078
[muted tone]

00:00:24.078 --> 00:00:25.997
[tone]

00:00:25.997 --> 00:00:27.999
This is my own
unique personal story.

00:00:27.999 --> 00:00:32.545
There are so many ways
 to be a deaf person.

00:00:32.545 --> 00:00:34.881
Some people
might be like me

00:00:34.881 --> 00:00:38.259
 and wear hearing aids
and lip read.

00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:40.803
Some might not wear
hearing aids at all

00:00:40.803 --> 00:00:43.056
and prefer sign language.

00:00:43.056 --> 00:00:45.600
(Is anyone here deaf or hard of hearing?)

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.561
(That’s you, Cece!
Raise your hand!)

00:00:48.561 --> 00:00:50.605
It’s very important for me
that the kids 

00:00:50.605 --> 00:00:52.357
who watch the show

00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:56.069
are experiencing it
 the way I experienced it.

00:00:56.069 --> 00:01:00.114
You can read the book
and get the sense of

00:01:00.114 --> 00:01:01.658
what speech is like,
for example,

00:01:01.658 --> 00:01:03.117
 from the speech balloons

00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:05.662
but you can watch the show
 and actually hear it.

00:01:05.662 --> 00:01:07.747
(Is it working?)

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:10.458
The audio design 
was really tough.

00:01:10.458 --> 00:01:12.418
I want to be able
to explain

00:01:12.418 --> 00:01:14.837
 to hearing people
what this is like.

00:01:14.837 --> 00:01:16.673
[muted speech]
(Hello, Cece.)

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:19.425
(Hello, Cece?)

00:01:19.425 --> 00:01:20.510
[muted speech]
(Yes!)

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.720
[muted speech]
(You listened and watched my lips)

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.389
[muted speech]
(and figured out what I said.)

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:26.265
If my character on the show

00:01:26.265 --> 00:01:28.851
 can see the person’s
mouth move

00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.562
then the audio
is pretty much clear.

00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:34.065
There's a little bit
of distortion because

00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:37.402
we're trying to
share with viewers

00:01:37.402 --> 00:01:40.822
what it might sound
 like to wear hearing aids.

00:01:40.822 --> 00:01:42.699
[loud whistle]

00:01:42.699 --> 00:01:43.741
[muted speech]
(Alright kids,)

00:01:43.741 --> 00:01:45.118
[muted speech]
(you know the drill.)

00:01:45.118 --> 00:01:46.536
[muted speech]
(Let’s break into two teams)

00:01:46.536 --> 00:01:48.162
[muted speech]
(for some major kickball action!)

00:01:48.162 --> 00:01:50.832
But then, there are
situations that happen

00:01:50.832 --> 00:01:54.252
where that comprehension
just goes away.

00:01:54.252 --> 00:01:56.337
Maybe the lights
get turned off 

00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:58.214
(Oh no.)

00:01:58.214 --> 00:02:00.383
or a character faces away.

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:02.885
(206 bones in the...)

00:02:02.885 --> 00:02:05.930
[inaudible speech]

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.932
And I think by recreating
 that experience,

00:02:07.932 --> 00:02:10.059
 it's sort of a reminder

00:02:10.059 --> 00:02:14.480
of how to be with a person
 that doesn't hear very well.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.357
You know, don't shout. 

00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:18.693
Remember that the person
needs to see your face.

00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:20.820
You know, be empathetic.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.864
Think about
what it would feel like.

00:02:22.864 --> 00:02:25.241
And this is a very direct way

00:02:25.241 --> 00:02:28.619
 to help kids understand
what that feels like.

